c({id:"U3:1",text:"U3 \u2014 FOAM's GUI Library\nU3 (package foam.u2) is FOAM's reactive GUI library. It builds real browser DOM directly \u2014 no virtual DOM diff, no templates, no compilation step. Every component is a live FOAM object whose properties drive the DOM reactively.\n\nThe name is historical: U1 was foam1's first UI library (foam.ui), which was also reactive, but template based. Google's security team didn't approve of templating because of the potential for XSS attacks if there were any bugs in the escaping code. To overcome this weakness, U2 was designed to to be DOM rather than template based, thus gaining the approval of the security team. At the time, the JS to C++ bridge in the browser was very slow, making a pure DOM-based solution inefficient. To combat this, U2 used a virtual-DOM which recreated the DOM model entirely on the JS side, and then batched updates to the C++/real-DOM side for improved performance. But in time, the browser engineers optimized this bridge, which made it so that virtual-DOM actually became slower than direct DOM manipulation. To then take advantage of this change, U3 was designed. It is mostly compatible with U2, but removeds the overhead and complexity of the virtual-DOM layer. It is now based on a wrapped-DOM design, which still adds extra features like reactivity, over top of the base DOM layer. The U3 code is still in the foam.u2 package because it is backward compatible and upgrading doesn't break existing code.\n\nAll U3 classes live under the foam.u2 package with foam.u2.Element2.js being the core file.\n\nU1 is only available in FOAM1.\nU2 is available in FOAM1 and FOAM2.\nU3 is the only available in FOAM2 and FOAM3.\n\n---",sourceId:"U3",kind:"flow",embeddingModel:"Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2",vector:[-0.0609649,0.011311395,-0.027509063,-0.04368506,0.11887949,-0.052215505,-0.054113414,-0.08202578,0.04179006,-0.028045347,-0.0067291074,-0.034998868,3.3922614E-5,-0.028898505,0.06450841,-0.036132585,0.03334787,-0.061